#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""The server agent: the analytics firewall for any Linux server, whatever
runs on it (Rails, Django, .NET, Go, Java, PHP...). It fetches the site's
rules (GET /events/firewall/rules, protocol in sdk/protocols/firewall.md)
and puts them where the server enforces them:

- nftables (default): a table of its own, `inet analytics_firewall`, that
  drops listed addresses on the web ports before anything else sees them
  (Docker's published ports included: it hooks in before Docker's NAT).
  Every listed address and range has a packet counter, so what was blocked
  (or, in monitor mode, would have been) is reported back.
- iptables: the same, for servers without nftables, as chains of its own
  (ANALYTICS-FW, ANALYTICS-FLOOD) in the mangle table's PREROUTING and ipsets
  (`analytics-list-*`, `analytics-flood-*`); needs iptables (legacy or nft)
  and ipset. "auto": nftables when nft works, else iptables, else none.
- nginx: allow/deny lines in a file nginx includes, reloaded after
  `nginx -t` passes. Nothing is reported, and monitor mode blocks nothing.
- none: no firewall, only file monitoring.

Flood protection (paid plans: `flood`, `origin` and `challenge` in the rules),
whatever the backend above, as far as this server has the pieces:

- nftables: a second table, `inet analytics_flood`: new connections per
  second and connections open at once per address (an IPv6 /64 counts as one)
  on the web ports, over which the address is banned for a while; and, with
  `origin`, the web ports only for Cloudflare and `allow`. iptables: the same
  in a chain of its own (hashlimit, connlimit and ipset ban sets).
- nginx, when it's installed: two files the owner includes, one in `http { }`
  (requests per second per address, and the page limits, `limits` in the rules,
  for every plan; answered 429; block mode only) and one in each
  `server { }` (the browser check: a page that sets a cookie with JavaScript).
  The agent reads nginx's error log and bans in the kernel what nginx limited.
- Any web server's access logs (Apache, LiteSpeed, OpenLiteSpeed, Caddy,
  Traefik, HAProxy, nginx), with the kernel's ban set (either backend): page requests per
  address counted as nginx's limit would, and an address over it banned in
  the kernel (block mode) or only reported (monitor mode).
- The browser check in Apache 2.4, LiteSpeed Enterprise, OpenLiteSpeed,
  Caddy 2 and HAProxy 2.2+: files of the agent's own, tested before each
  reload, which the owner includes (Apache: nothing to add). Traefik can't
  answer a request by itself, so it gets the access log limit only.

File monitoring (protocol in sdk/protocols/files.md): about once an hour, in
the background, the site's files ("files_roots" in the config) are compared
with the version you accepted, and what's different is reported: changed
code, backdoors, defacement. The record lives in the state folder (root
only), out of the site's reach. After a deploy, `analytics-agent accept`.
More sites on the same server: `analytics-agent add-site SITE_ID /their/folder`
(their files are reported to their own site; the firewall follows the main one).

How the server is set up (protocol in sdk/protocols/posture.md): once a day,
also in the background, the settings that leave it open (sshd, automatic
updates, ports open to the internet, the Docker socket, code anyone can
write), the scheduled job files and SSH keys a break-in hides in to come back,
and files in the web folder that should never be public. It only reads, and it
sends no file's contents: paths, hashes, sizes, times and key fingerprints.

How the machine is doing (protocol in sdk/protocols/resources.md): CPU, load,
memory, swap and disk space, sampled every minute on a thread of its own and
reported every few minutes, so the dashboard can show what you would otherwise
install node_exporter, Prometheus and Grafana to see. A reading this machine
won't give is left out rather than sent as a zero.

What fills the disks (protocol in sdk/protocols/disk-usage.md): every three
hours, in a child process at the lowest CPU and disk priority, the sizes of a
fixed list of places (logs, the journal, Docker, package caches, backups,
databases...), what of it can be cleared, the biggest files and the deleted
files a process still holds open. Folder listings and sizes only: it reads no
file's contents and deletes nothing.

Its databases and queues (protocols in sdk/protocols/databases.md and
queues.md): every minute, on a thread of its own, each database listed under
"databases" in the config (PostgreSQL, MySQL/MariaDB, ClickHouse, MongoDB),
as it sees itself: the statements that cost it the most time and the rows
each read, the tables read end to end, connections and lock waits; and the
backlog of each RabbitMQ listed under "queues". It only reads statistics the
database already keeps, and every statement loses its values before it's sent.

What's deployed (protocol in sdk/protocols/deploys.md, POST /events/apps): every
five minutes, on a thread of its own, the commit each git work tree under the
site's folders is on, and (for the main site) the image each running Docker
container came from. A changed HEAD or image is a deploy marker on the charts,
and an hourly `git ls-remote` says whether the folder is behind its branch. It
only reads: git runs as the folder's owner, never prompts and never fetches.

Whether its services run (protocol in sdk/protocols/services.md, POST
/events/services): every minute, on a thread of its own, each service listed
under "services" in the config (a port, an address, a systemd unit, a process)
and the common ones it finds (database, queue and web server units, Docker's
containers), so Health → All systems can give each a light.

Backups (protocol in sdk/protocols/backups.md, POST /events/backups): every
night, on a thread of its own, each database under "backups" dumped with its
own tool (PostgreSQL, MySQL/MariaDB, MongoDB, SQLite; on the server or in a
Docker container) and each folder, through restic into your own storage,
encrypted here with a password only you have. Health → Backups shows them, a
missed or failed one is an alert, and a part can be downloaded or put back:
from the dashboard only when "allow_restore" is true here, after copying how
things are now, and undone if anything fails.

The `rate` limit isn't enforced here (the kernel sees packets, not requests);
requests are limited by nginx with `flood` instead, when its file is included.
Python 3.6+, standard library only. Runs as root (nftables and iptables need it).

    analytics-agent run       keep the rules in place (what the service runs)
    analytics-agent once      fetch and apply once
    analytics-agent status    what's in place now
    analytics-agent clear     remove everything the agent put in place
    analytics-agent files [SITE_ID]    check the files now and report what changed
    analytics-agent posture [SITE_ID]  check how the server is set up now and report it
    analytics-agent resources          sample the machine now, print it and report it
    analytics-agent disk               measure what fills the disks now, print it and report it
    analytics-agent apps [SITE_ID]     list the apps deployed here now and report them
    analytics-agent services           check the services now, print them and report them
    analytics-agent databases          read the listed databases and queues now (twice, 10 s apart), print and report it
    analytics-agent accept [SITE_ID]   the files as they are now are the accepted version (after a deploy)
    analytics-agent add-site SITE_ID FOLDER...   watch another site's files on this server
    analytics-agent remove-site SITE_ID          stop watching it
    analytics-agent backup-setup       install restic, make the backups' password and repository
    analytics-agent backup             back up now
    analytics-agent backups            list the backups in the repository
    analytics-agent restore RUN [PART...] [--into FOLDER] [--yes]
                                       put a backup back (all of it, or the parts named); --into copies it out instead
    analytics-agent export RUN PART FILE         write one part of a backup to a file
"""
